    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The deep connection between the Uncertainty Principle and wave-particle duality provides the most intuitive physical understanding. In quantum mechanics, particles like electrons are described by wave functions, $\\Psi$, and their physical properties correspond to the momentum and position distributions derived from these waves (via Fourier transforms). A state with a perfectly defined momentum corresponds to a pure sine wave, which extends infinitely in space, meaning its position is completely uncertain ($\\Delta x = \\infty$). Conversely, a perfectly localized particle, represented by a sharp wave packet ($\\Delta x$ is very small), must be constructed by superimposing many different momentum waves. Mathematically, the Fourier transform dictates that a sharper localization in position results in a broader, more spread-out spectrum of momenta, manifesting the inherent trade-off $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\geq \\frac{\\hbar}{2}$. This mathematical necessity ensures that the wave nature—which intrinsically links spatial extent and frequency content—governs the limits of simultaneous knowledge.",
      "source": "target",
